  "observations": [
    {
      "area": "Bounds and first load",
      "result": "Both the helper and original decoded routine start by reading buffer.len, casting usize to u64 with wrap and comparing the same u64 position >= length. The helper takes that branch directly to the original routine without loading a byte. At an in-bounds position, the same usize cast and one-byte load occur first."
    },
    {
      "area": "Fast-path equivalence",
      "result": "The selected set is exactly 32 <= byte < 128 excluding quote 34 and backslash 92. In the original routine those bytes pass the quote, control and escape branches, then take its ASCII return. The helper returns the same int.add.wrap(position,1), int.shl.checked(...,32) and bit.or(...,byte). It introduces no truncation, saturating operation or host numeric calculation into the HIA value path."
    },
    {
      "area": "Overflow and checked shift",
      "result": "Original and helper use the same unsigned wrap increment and checked shift opcode/constant 32. The original performs the increment earlier than the helper on the fast path, but the intervening operations are nontrapping comparisons/boolean operations and make no writes. The exact shift/trap semantics therefore remain those of the original opcode; this review does not substitute JavaScript arithmetic for HIA execution."
    },
    {
      "area": "Quote, escapes, controls, UTF-8 and end-of-input",
      "result": "All excluded bytes call the exact original reader with the original readbuf and position. No continuation-byte, escape, surrogate, sentinel or error logic is replaced. Fallback adds one immutable in-bounds byte load, while out-of-bounds fallback adds no load. These statements assume the existing immutable private readbuf lease, not an arbitrary concurrently mutated or invalid foreign pointer."
    },
    {
      "area": "Caller write order",
      "result": "The entire decode_string function differs at one call target only, retaining the same argument SSA identifiers and result identifier. No output/workspace writes or mutable cursor operations are moved. Every other original function, including the complete unicode reader, is byte-identical in each input/candidate pair."
    },
    {
      "area": "SSA and control flow",
      "result": "The appended helper has four blocks b0..b3, 23 operations and exactly one definition of each block/local v0..v31. The branch argument types match the destinations; the slow return passes v29/v30 unchanged to the original reader. Existing function numbering is contiguous f0..fN and the helper is fresh f(N+1). Function/block text is not a substitute for the original parser/compiler qualification."
    },
    {
      "area": "Source binding",
      "result": "All 14 input/candidate texts were fetched and independently checked by full UTF-8 byte count, SHA256 and Git blob SHA. Removing the exact 1060-byte appended helper and restoring the one call line reconstructs each entire inline source byte for byte. The seven original unicode readers are identical after function-name alpha normalization."
    },
    {
      "area": "Limits",
      "result": "Independent text counts match the supplied per-role profile. The largest role27 has 79 functions, 1445 blocks, 6901 operations and maximum SSA suffix 2000; all original profile gates are preserved. The original full 329-case runtime and compilation qualification remain required."
    }
